//! The File module: opening, reading, writing and closing a file, with every failure receivable.
//!
//! Written in Jairs. PLAN §8.3 item 5, and the module the error model was holding up: every routine here can
//! fail for a reason the caller has to handle, so before `#must` (ADR-0151) there was no way to say that
//! ignoring the failure is an error rather than a style choice.
//!
//! # Why descriptors rather than a `File` struct with an allocator
//!
//! A handle here is a **plain `s64` descriptor** in a one-field struct, not a buffered stream. `read` and
//! `write` are the syscalls, byte counts and all, and a caller who wants buffering builds it on top.
//!
//! The alternative — a `File` owning a buffer — has to answer where the buffer comes from, when it flushes,
//! and what happens to unflushed bytes when the handle is dropped. There are no destructors (ADR-0008), so
//! the answer to the last one would be "they are lost unless you remember to flush", which is a worse
//! bargain than "you called `write` and it wrote". Buffering is a real feature and it belongs to a module
//! that says so in its name.
//!
//! # Why every routine is `#must` except `close`
//!
//! `open`, `read`, `write` and `seek` return a value beside a success flag, and the flag is `#must`: a
//! caller who ignores it reads zero bytes from a file that failed to open and gets a silently empty result,
//! which is exactly the bug the marker exists to prevent. `_ = f();` is the escape hatch when a caller
//! genuinely does not care, and writing it is the point.
//!
//! `close` is **not** `#must`, and that asymmetry is deliberate rather than an oversight. A failing `close`
//! means buffered data was lost — but this module does not buffer, so there is nothing to lose; and a caller
//! who checks it has no recovery available, because the descriptor is gone either way. It returns the status
//! for a caller who wants to log it, and does not insist.
//!
//! # What is absent, and why
//!
//! **No `errno`.** A failure says *that* it failed, not *why*. Reading `errno` needs a thread-local, which
//! this language has no concept of (`Thread` is W11), and `__error` / `__errno_location` differ by platform
//! in both name and shape. A wrong `errno` is worse than none — it names a cause that is not the cause —
//! and the flag is enough to decide whether to carry on.
//!
//! **No paths as a type.** A path is a `string`, and `File_Utilities` has the joining and splitting. This
//! module's business is the descriptor.
//!
//! **No comptime file reading.** `#foreign` is refused at compile time (ADR-0006), so a `#run` cannot read a
//! file — which is a real limitation for a build script and is the compiler's decision, not this module's.

#import "Basic";

/// An open file.
///
/// A struct rather than a bare `s64` so that a descriptor cannot be passed where a count is wanted: the two
/// are both integers and the compiler will not confuse them once one has a name.
File :: struct {
    /// The OS descriptor. Negative when the file is not open.
    fd: s64;
}

/// Open for reading only.
READ :: 0;

/// Open for writing only.
WRITE :: 1;

/// Open for reading and writing.
READ_WRITE :: 2;

/// Create the file if it does not exist. Combine with `WRITE` or `READ_WRITE`.
///
/// **Selected for the target** (ADR-0184 §6), where it used to be macOS's number under a comment admitting
/// so: `O_CREAT` is 0x200 on macOS and 0x40 on Linux. The three access modes above *are* portable — 0, 1, 2
/// everywhere — which is why only these three flags need selecting.
///
/// **This is the flag whose being wrong is worst.** ADR-0157 §2 measured a mis-declared `open` producing a
/// file with permissions `---------x`: no diagnostic, a plausible-looking result, unreadable. A wrong
/// `O_CREAT` is the same failure shape one step earlier — the call succeeds and does something else.
CREATE :: #run create_flag();

/// Truncate the file to nothing on open. Combine with `WRITE` or `READ_WRITE`.
///
/// 0x400 on macOS, 0x200 on Linux — selected for the same reason `CREATE` is.
TRUNCATE :: #run truncate_flag();

/// Append every write to the end. Combine with `WRITE` or `READ_WRITE`.
///
/// 0x8 on macOS, 0x400 on Linux — selected for the same reason.
APPEND :: #run append_flag();

/// Default permissions for a created file: `0644`, owner-writable and world-readable.
///
/// Octal 644 is decimal 420. Written as decimal because this language has no octal literal, which is exactly
/// the kind of detail that makes a hand-computed permission wrong — so it is a named constant computed once.
CREATE_MODE :: 420;

/// Seek from the start of the file.
SEEK_SET :: 0;

/// Seek from the current position.
SEEK_CURRENT :: 1;

/// Seek from the end of the file.
SEEK_END :: 2;

/// `O_CREAT` for the target (ADR-0184 §6).
///
/// A procedure a `#run` evaluates, which is the file-scope idiom `modules/Random`'s
/// `GOLDEN :: #run golden_seed();` established and ADR-0181 used for `CLOCK_MONOTONIC`. No `#if` and no
/// generated declaration: the *value* differs and nothing else does, so a value is what is selected.
///
/// **Windows gets Linux's number and that is a placeholder, not a fact.** Windows has no POSIX `open`
/// flags at all — its `_open` uses `_O_CREAT` = 0x100 — and this module's `#foreign` bindings name POSIX
/// symbols that a Windows libc does not export under these names. So a Windows build fails to *link*
/// rather than opening a file with the wrong flag, which is the failure mode to prefer: naming a number
/// that would let a wrong call succeed is what ADR-0181 §2 refused to do for `clock_gettime`.
create_flag :: () -> s64 {
    if os() == Operating_System.MACOS {
        return 512;
    }
    return 64;
}

/// `O_TRUNC` for the target — 0x400 on macOS, 0x200 on Linux (ADR-0184 §6).
truncate_flag :: () -> s64 {
    if os() == Operating_System.MACOS {
        return 1024;
    }
    return 512;
}

/// `O_APPEND` for the target — 0x8 on macOS, 0x400 on Linux (ADR-0184 §6).
append_flag :: () -> s64 {
    if os() == Operating_System.MACOS {
        return 8;
    }
    return 1024;
}

/// libc `open(2)`, called with **only its fixed arguments**.
///
/// C declares `open(const char *, int, ...)` and reads a third `mode_t` only when `O_CREAT` is set. Calling a
/// variadic function with just its fixed arguments is well-defined on every ABI, so this binding is correct
/// — and it is the only correct one available, because **a fixed-arity declaration of a variadic function
/// passes the extra argument in the wrong place**. That was probed, not assumed: declaring
/// `open(path, flags, mode)` and calling it created a file with permissions `---------x` on arm64 macOS,
/// where variadic arguments go on the stack while a fixed third argument goes in a register. No diagnostic,
/// a plausible-looking file, and unreadable — the silent-wrong-answer shape this project refuses.
///
/// So creation goes through `creat_raw` instead, which is genuinely fixed-arity. PLAN §8.5 lists C-variadic
/// foreign calls as unbuilt; this module is the first to *need* them and the workaround is recorded here
/// rather than in a comment nobody reads (ADR-0157 §2).
open_raw :: (path: *u8, flags: s64) -> s64 #foreign libc "open";

/// libc `creat(2)`: `open(path, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, mode)`, and **not variadic**.
///
/// The one portable way to create a file with a known mode without a variadic call. Its fixed shape is the
/// whole reason it is used, which is why it is here rather than `open` with three arguments.
creat_raw :: (path: *u8, mode: s64) -> s64 #foreign libc "creat";

/// libc `close(2)`.
close_raw :: (fd: s64) -> s64 #foreign libc "close";

/// libc `read(2)`.
read_raw :: (fd: s64, buf: *u8, count: s64) -> s64 #foreign libc "read";

/// libc `write(2)`.
write_raw :: (fd: s64, buf: *u8, count: s64) -> s64 #foreign libc "write";

/// libc `lseek(2)`.
lseek_raw :: (fd: s64, offset: s64, whence: s64) -> s64 #foreign libc "lseek";

/// libc `unlink(2)`.
unlink_raw :: (path: *u8) -> s64 #foreign libc "unlink";

/// Opens `path` with `flags`, creating it with `CREATE_MODE` when `CREATE` is set.
///
/// `#must`: a caller who ignores the flag holds a closed file and every later call fails quietly.
///
/// The path is copied into a NUL-terminated scratch buffer from **temporary storage**, because a Jairs
/// `string` is data and a count with no terminator, and `open` reads to a NUL. The copy is dead the moment
/// the call returns, which is exactly what an arena models (ADR-0065) — the same reason `JSON`'s `strtod`
/// scratch comes from there (ADR-0156 §2).
open :: (path: string, flags: s64) -> (File, bool) #must {
    f: File;
    f.fd = -1;
    c_path, ok := nul_terminated(path);
    if !ok {
        return f, false;
    }
    // **Creation is separated out**, because the mode cannot be passed to `open` — see `open_raw`. The
    // three shapes, in the order they are cheapest:
    //
    //   * no `CREATE`            one `open`, nothing to arrange;
    //   * `CREATE` + `TRUNCATE`  one `creat`, which *is* write+create+truncate — then a reopen only when
    //                            the caller wanted read access too, since `creat` gives write-only;
    //   * `CREATE` alone         try `open` first, and only `creat` when the file is missing, so an
    //                            existing file's contents survive — which is what `APPEND` means.
    if flags % (CREATE * 2) < CREATE {
        fd := open_raw(c_path, flags);
        if fd < 0 {
            return f, false;
        }
        f.fd = fd;
        return f, true;
    }
    without_create := flags - CREATE;
    if without_create % (TRUNCATE * 2) >= TRUNCATE {
        made := creat_raw(c_path, CREATE_MODE);
        if made < 0 {
            return f, false;
        }
        // `creat` is write-only. When the caller asked for read access as well, close it and reopen — the
        // file exists now, so no create flag is needed and no mode has to travel.
        if without_create - TRUNCATE == WRITE {
            f.fd = made;
            return f, true;
        }
        _ = close_raw(made);
        fd := open_raw(c_path, without_create);
        if fd < 0 {
            return f, false;
        }
        f.fd = fd;
        return f, true;
    }
    fd := open_raw(c_path, without_create);
    if fd < 0 {
        made := creat_raw(c_path, CREATE_MODE);
        if made < 0 {
            return f, false;
        }
        _ = close_raw(made);
        fd = open_raw(c_path, without_create);
        if fd < 0 {
            return f, false;
        }
    }
    f.fd = fd;
    return f, true;
}

/// Opens `path` for reading.
///
/// The common case, named so a caller does not have to remember that `READ` is 0.
open_read :: (path: string) -> (File, bool) #must {
    return open(path, READ);
}

/// Opens `path` for writing, creating it and truncating it.
///
/// Truncating, because "open for writing" almost always means "replace the contents", and a caller who wants
/// to keep them wants `open_append` or an explicit `open` with their own flags. Making the common case
/// explicit here is better than making every caller compose three constants correctly.
open_write :: (path: string) -> (File, bool) #must {
    return open(path, WRITE + CREATE + TRUNCATE);
}

/// Opens `path` for appending, creating it if it does not exist.
open_append :: (path: string) -> (File, bool) #must {
    return open(path, WRITE + CREATE + APPEND);
}

/// Closes `f` and marks it closed. Returns the OS status.
///
/// Not `#must` — see the module docs. Safe on an already-closed file, so a caller can close on every path
/// without tracking whether they already did, which is what makes it usable where a `defer` would go.
close :: (f: *File) -> s64 {
    if f.fd < 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    status := close_raw(f.fd);
    f.fd = -1;
    return status;
}

/// Whether `f` is open.
is_open :: (f: *File) -> bool {
    return f.fd >= 0;
}

/// Reads up to `buffer.count` bytes into `buffer`, returning how many and whether the call succeeded.
///
/// **A short read is success, not failure**, and zero bytes means end of file rather than an error. That is
/// the syscall's contract and hiding it would be wrong: a caller reading a pipe or a socket has to loop
/// either way, and a wrapper that looped for them would block where the caller wanted a partial answer.
/// `read_all` below is the looping version, for the case where a caller does want it.
read :: (f: *File, buffer: []u8) -> (s64, bool) #must {
    if f.fd < 0 {
        return 0, false;
    }
    if buffer.count == 0 {
        return 0, true;
    }
    // `*buffer[0]` rather than `buffer.data`, because a view deliberately has **no** `.data` field
    // (ADR-0044 §4: it would hand out an unbounded pointer). The zero-count case is returned above, so
    // element 0 exists and the index is in bounds.
    n := read_raw(f.fd, *buffer[0], buffer.count);
    if n < 0 {
        return 0, false;
    }
    return n, true;
}

/// Writes `bytes`, returning how many were written and whether the call succeeded.
///
/// A short write is success here too, for the same reason, and `write_all` is the looping version.
write :: (f: *File, bytes: []u8) -> (s64, bool) #must {
    if f.fd < 0 {
        return 0, false;
    }
    if bytes.count == 0 {
        return 0, true;
    }
    n := write_raw(f.fd, *bytes[0], bytes.count);
    if n < 0 {
        return 0, false;
    }
    return n, true;
}

/// Writes every byte of `bytes`, looping over short writes.
///
/// Returns whether all of them landed. **This is the routine most callers want**, because a short write on a
/// regular file is rare enough that a caller who does not loop has a bug that appears under load and never
/// in a test — which is the argument for offering it rather than leaving everyone to write the loop.
write_all :: (f: *File, bytes: []u8) -> bool #must {
    at := 0;
    while at < bytes.count {
        n, ok := write(f, view(*bytes[at], bytes.count - at));
        if !ok {
            return false;
        }
        // A zero-byte write with no error would spin forever. It should not happen on a regular file, and
        // "should not happen" is exactly what a loop condition must not rely on.
        if n == 0 {
            return false;
        }
        at = at + n;
    }
    return true;
}

/// Writes a string's bytes.
///
/// A convenience over `write_all`, because a string is the thing a caller usually has and building the view
/// by hand at every call site is noise.
write_string :: (f: *File, text: string) -> bool #must {
    // A `string` *does* have `.data` — it is a distinct type from a view, and its pointer is what makes it
    // passable to a foreign call at all (ADR-0044 §4 draws the line between the two here).
    return write_all(f, view(text.data, text.count));
}

/// Fills `buffer` as far as it can, looping over short reads, and returns how many bytes it got.
///
/// Stops at end of file, so a return less than `buffer.count` means the file ended — which a caller can
/// distinguish from a partial read, unlike `read`'s answer.
read_all :: (f: *File, buffer: []u8) -> (s64, bool) #must {
    at := 0;
    while at < buffer.count {
        n, ok := read(f, view(*buffer[at], buffer.count - at));
        if !ok {
            return at, false;
        }
        if n == 0 {
            return at, true;
        }
        at = at + n;
    }
    return at, true;
}

/// Moves the read/write position and returns the new absolute offset.
seek :: (f: *File, offset: s64, whence: s64) -> (s64, bool) #must {
    if f.fd < 0 {
        return 0, false;
    }
    at := lseek_raw(f.fd, offset, whence);
    if at < 0 {
        return 0, false;
    }
    return at, true;
}

/// The file's size in bytes, leaving the position where it was.
///
/// Two seeks rather than a `stat`, which would need a `struct stat` **by value across the FFI boundary** —
/// E0286 refuses that today (ADR-0150) and it is W10's hard gate (PLAN §8.1.2). So the size comes from
/// seeking to the end and back, which works on any seekable file and is the honest shape until an aggregate
/// can cross. Recorded because `stat` is what a reader would expect.
size :: (f: *File) -> (s64, bool) #must {
    here, here_ok := seek(f, 0, SEEK_CURRENT);
    if !here_ok {
        return 0, false;
    }
    end, end_ok := seek(f, 0, SEEK_END);
    if !end_ok {
        return 0, false;
    }
    _, back_ok := seek(f, here, SEEK_SET);
    if !back_ok {
        return 0, false;
    }
    return end, true;
}

/// Deletes `path`. Returns whether it went.
///
/// `#must`, unlike `close`: a caller who deletes a temporary file and ignores the failure leaves it behind,
/// and the next run sees stale data — a real bug with no local symptom, which is the shape the marker is for.
remove :: (path: string) -> bool #must {
    c_path, ok := nul_terminated(path);
    if !ok {
        return false;
    }
    return unlink_raw(c_path) == 0;
}

/// Whether `path` can be opened for reading.
///
/// **Not** an `access(2)` or `stat` check, and the difference matters: this answers "could I read it", which
/// is the question a caller is really asking, rather than "does something exist there" — a directory exists
/// and cannot be read, and a file can exist and be unreadable. It costs an open and a close.
///
/// Not `#must`: it *is* the answer, so there is no second value to ignore.
exists :: (path: string) -> bool {
    f, ok := open_read(path);
    if !ok {
        return false;
    }
    _ = close(*f);
    return true;
}

#scope_module

/// Copies `path` into NUL-terminated scratch from temporary storage.
///
/// Module-private because the pointer is only valid until the next `reset_temporary_storage`, which is a
/// contract that belongs inside this module rather than in its surface.
nul_terminated :: (path: string) -> (*u8, bool) {
    scratch := talloc(path.count + 1);
    if scratch == null {
        return null, false;
    }
    bytes := view(scratch, path.count + 1);
    i := 0;
    while i < path.count {
        bytes[i] = cast(u8, byte_at_raw(path, i));
        i = i + 1;
    }
    bytes[path.count] = cast(u8, 0);
    return scratch, true;
}

/// One byte of a string, without importing `String` for it.
///
/// `File` imports only `Basic`, deliberately: it sits at the bottom of the library beside it, and importing
/// `String` here would make every program that opens a file pull in the string routines. One three-line
/// duplicate is cheaper than that coupling, and it is the same judgement `String` itself makes about
/// `copy_bytes`.
byte_at_raw :: (s: string, index: s64) -> s64 {
    return cast(s64, (s.data + index).*);
}
